WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.785 --> 00:00:08.410
SUDHIR HASBE: Welcome, everyone,
to the Smart Analytics Roadmap

00:00:08.410 --> 00:00:09.240
session.

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:10.280
I'm Sudhir Hasbe.

00:00:10.280 --> 00:00:13.390
I'm the Director of Product
for Smart Analytics at Google

00:00:13.390 --> 00:00:15.140
Cloud.

00:00:15.140 --> 00:00:17.880
Data across
organizations is growing.

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:22.520
And then organizations need a
very strong analytics platform

00:00:22.520 --> 00:00:25.250
to leverage this
data, create insights,

00:00:25.250 --> 00:00:28.220
and make real-time decisions
on top of this data.

00:00:28.220 --> 00:00:30.380
At Google Cloud, we
are a data company.

00:00:30.380 --> 00:00:33.020
We basically collect
massive amounts of data.

00:00:33.020 --> 00:00:35.690
We leverage our machine
learning and AI capabilities

00:00:35.690 --> 00:00:39.440
to generate insights from
them and power our products.

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:41.150
We have more than
nine products--

00:00:41.150 --> 00:00:45.470
we have nine products that have
more than a billion users that

00:00:45.470 --> 00:00:47.940
use it every day.

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:49.520
At Google Cloud,
we have also seen

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:53.610
convergence of a lot of
these technology trends.

00:00:53.610 --> 00:00:56.430
We've seen organizations
are breaking down

00:00:56.430 --> 00:01:00.590
data silos between data
lakes and data warehouses.

00:01:00.590 --> 00:01:02.780
As organizations
are moving to cloud,

00:01:02.780 --> 00:01:04.610
they want to be
able to interoperate

00:01:04.610 --> 00:01:08.630
between all of these data
assets that have been created.

00:01:08.630 --> 00:01:11.210
Second, we have
seen organizations

00:01:11.210 --> 00:01:14.270
focused a lot more on
real-time decision-making.

00:01:14.270 --> 00:01:16.820
And lot of processes
historically

00:01:16.820 --> 00:01:18.920
have been built for
batch processing.

00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:21.620
And this transition to
real-time processing

00:01:21.620 --> 00:01:25.220
is requiring organizations
to think about,

00:01:25.220 --> 00:01:28.280
how do you go ahead and build
consolidated solutions that

00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:31.550
can run on both real time
as well as batch data?

00:01:31.550 --> 00:01:35.600
And then, finally, analytics
and AI, merging these concepts,

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:39.260
data scientists are
spending 80% in some studies

00:01:39.260 --> 00:01:41.200
on data preparation.

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:45.350
And analysts are trying to go
in and do predictive analytics

00:01:45.350 --> 00:01:46.870
on top of data that they have.

00:01:46.870 --> 00:01:50.870
And convergence of
these different users

00:01:50.870 --> 00:01:55.340
within organizations, use cases
is also becoming a norm now.

00:01:55.340 --> 00:01:59.450
So at Google Cloud, our vision
is to go ahead and provide you

00:01:59.450 --> 00:02:02.480
with an enterprise-class
data platform that

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:06.860
is proven to be dependable.

00:02:06.860 --> 00:02:09.440
We at Google actually
have services

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:12.960
that are built for scale,
built for availability.

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:15.550
So we want to bring
that same kind of record

00:02:15.550 --> 00:02:18.330
to our data platform.

00:02:18.330 --> 00:02:24.050
The other thing is we believe in
open and flexible capabilities.

00:02:24.050 --> 00:02:27.890
We are also leveraging some of
the intelligence capabilities

00:02:27.890 --> 00:02:30.890
with AI/ML, embedding
it within our products.

00:02:30.890 --> 00:02:33.830
So we want to provide you with
this end-to-end Smart Analytics

00:02:33.830 --> 00:02:38.090
platform that allows you to go
ahead and get more value out

00:02:38.090 --> 00:02:39.270
of your data.

00:02:39.270 --> 00:02:41.330
So what does the
platform entail?

00:02:41.330 --> 00:02:44.150
You can go in and collect
massive amounts of events

00:02:44.150 --> 00:02:47.540
in real time or in
batch with the platform.

00:02:47.540 --> 00:02:51.230
Organizations like Spotify are
collecting events every time

00:02:51.230 --> 00:02:52.130
you play a song.

00:02:52.130 --> 00:02:56.260
The event gets collected
across the globe using Pub/Sub.

00:02:56.260 --> 00:02:59.330
They process the data
through Dataflow,

00:02:59.330 --> 00:03:03.398
which is a streaming
service that

00:03:03.398 --> 00:03:05.690
allows you to go ahead and
do stream processing as well

00:03:05.690 --> 00:03:07.220
as batch processing.

00:03:07.220 --> 00:03:08.990
You could also use
Dataproc, which

00:03:08.990 --> 00:03:12.060
is a managed Hadoop
and Spark environment

00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:14.060
with similar kind of data sets.

00:03:14.060 --> 00:03:16.940
And then you can use BigQuery
for large-scale analysis.

00:03:16.940 --> 00:03:20.270
In addition to that, we have
launched migration service

00:03:20.270 --> 00:03:21.830
that allows you
to easily migrate

00:03:21.830 --> 00:03:24.860
from your traditional data
warehouses to BigQuery.

00:03:24.860 --> 00:03:26.690
We launched Data Fusion.

00:03:26.690 --> 00:03:28.790
It's a data integration service.

00:03:28.790 --> 00:03:32.000
Visually, you can create
data pipelines end to end

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:36.620
and then go ahead
and deploy it on GCP.

00:03:36.620 --> 00:03:39.080
We went GA end of last year.

00:03:39.080 --> 00:03:40.580
And we have seen
tremendous interest

00:03:40.580 --> 00:03:43.600
in this from customers
across the globe.

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:46.070
Dataprep is a data
wrangling service.

00:03:46.070 --> 00:03:47.900
It's a partnership
with Trifacta.

00:03:47.900 --> 00:03:52.970
It allows you to go ahead and do
zero-code data wrangling, data

00:03:52.970 --> 00:03:54.300
preparation.

00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:56.510
Now, we are also going to
have a couple of new SKUs

00:03:56.510 --> 00:04:00.890
that are being launched
for Dataprep as we

00:04:00.890 --> 00:04:03.330
go into this year.

00:04:03.330 --> 00:04:04.610
The other thing is also--

00:04:04.610 --> 00:04:06.170
now, from a storage
perspective, you

00:04:06.170 --> 00:04:08.150
can leverage
BigQuery or Bigtable.

00:04:08.150 --> 00:04:11.330
Lot of organizations
use Bigtable

00:04:11.330 --> 00:04:16.412
as their backbone for
high-throughput data sets,

00:04:16.412 --> 00:04:18.829
like when you need it for your
e-commerce website and all.

00:04:18.829 --> 00:04:23.740
And organizations use BigQuery
for large-scale analytical data

00:04:23.740 --> 00:04:24.240
sets.

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:26.660
It's a analytical store.

00:04:26.660 --> 00:04:30.980
And then you can use BigQuery
for analysis with SQL.

00:04:30.980 --> 00:04:33.860
It has various different
capabilities in SQL.

00:04:33.860 --> 00:04:36.980
And then things like
BI Engine, which

00:04:36.980 --> 00:04:40.902
is our in-memory technology,
embedded machine learning.

00:04:40.902 --> 00:04:42.610
So you can create
machine learning models

00:04:42.610 --> 00:04:44.630
with just a couple
of lines of SQL.

00:04:44.630 --> 00:04:48.170
And then you can also
use Spark with Dataproc.

00:04:48.170 --> 00:04:50.120
A lot of organizations
like using

00:04:50.120 --> 00:04:52.400
Spark ML for machine
learning models and all.

00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:54.390
You can do that with Dataproc.

00:04:54.390 --> 00:04:57.530
And then, finally, to
activate, to take actions

00:04:57.530 --> 00:05:00.748
on top of your data,
you can use Looker.

00:05:00.748 --> 00:05:02.540
Looker is a great
addition to our platform.

00:05:02.540 --> 00:05:06.770
We'll talk more about that
later in the presentation.

00:05:06.770 --> 00:05:10.250
Connected Sheets-- you can
now leverage Connected Sheets,

00:05:10.250 --> 00:05:14.425
like Google Sheets, to
leverage the power of BigQuery

00:05:14.425 --> 00:05:16.270
on the back end.

00:05:16.270 --> 00:05:17.860
You can take a
petabyte-scale data set

00:05:17.860 --> 00:05:20.830
and just start analyzing
it with Connected Sheets.

00:05:20.830 --> 00:05:22.710
And then on top of
that, we launched Data

00:05:22.710 --> 00:05:24.980
Catalog earlier in the year.

00:05:24.980 --> 00:05:28.360
It allows you to go in and
get better data, understanding

00:05:28.360 --> 00:05:29.860
of your environment.

00:05:29.860 --> 00:05:32.560
You can search for any
data set that you have,

00:05:32.560 --> 00:05:35.440
put some policies
on top of that data.

00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:37.690
And then Composer, which is
our workflow orchestration

00:05:37.690 --> 00:05:40.720
capability, which
has managed Airflow,

00:05:40.720 --> 00:05:43.670
allows you to bring all of
these services together.

00:05:43.670 --> 00:05:46.630
So overall, if you look at
the platform, you can collect,

00:05:46.630 --> 00:05:47.890
you can process the data.

00:05:47.890 --> 00:05:52.570
You can store it in various
different platform solutions

00:05:52.570 --> 00:05:54.760
that we have for
different use cases.

00:05:54.760 --> 00:05:56.650
You can analyze it at
scale, and then you

00:05:56.650 --> 00:06:00.040
can activate on top of that
data the different kind

00:06:00.040 --> 00:06:01.900
of activations.

00:06:01.900 --> 00:06:04.720
One of the other things we
were talking about earlier

00:06:04.720 --> 00:06:06.250
is this interoperability.

00:06:06.250 --> 00:06:08.980
So at Google Cloud, one of
our key value propositions

00:06:08.980 --> 00:06:11.980
is to go ahead and have this
completely interoperable

00:06:11.980 --> 00:06:15.430
environment, where you can have
your data in a data warehouse

00:06:15.430 --> 00:06:17.890
or in data lake or
in databases, and you

00:06:17.890 --> 00:06:21.310
can use the same BigQuery engine
to go ahead and run queries

00:06:21.310 --> 00:06:24.490
across all of them,
even Google Sheets.

00:06:24.490 --> 00:06:26.710
Then on the other side,
if you store your data

00:06:26.710 --> 00:06:29.830
in BigQuery, which is a
highly analytical store,

00:06:29.830 --> 00:06:32.657
you can then use any
of the other engines,

00:06:32.657 --> 00:06:34.740
including-- like, you can
use the BigQuery engine,

00:06:34.740 --> 00:06:36.850
but you can also use Spark
engine with Dataproc.

00:06:36.850 --> 00:06:38.740
You can use Beam with Dataflow.

00:06:38.740 --> 00:06:40.000
You can use Cloud AI.

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:42.370
So all of these different
engines, like AutoML and all,

00:06:42.370 --> 00:06:44.440
they seamlessly work
on Cloud or BigQuery.

00:06:44.440 --> 00:06:47.980
So this interoperability is one
of the key value propositions

00:06:47.980 --> 00:06:49.180
that we are focused on.

00:06:49.180 --> 00:06:51.070
We have added a lot of
capabilities in the last year

00:06:51.070 --> 00:06:51.570
or so.

00:06:51.570 --> 00:06:54.460
And then we continue
to improve it as we go.

00:06:54.460 --> 00:06:56.860
We have seen tremendous
growth across the globe.

00:06:56.860 --> 00:07:00.760
Every industry, every
region, organizations

00:07:00.760 --> 00:07:05.540
like from Home Depot
to AirAsia, HSBC, UPS--

00:07:05.540 --> 00:07:09.500
like, you will see different
kind of organizations.

00:07:09.500 --> 00:07:14.120
Some of them like cloud native
startups, to Twitters and all,

00:07:14.120 --> 00:07:17.530
and to traditional
enterprise organizations

00:07:17.530 --> 00:07:19.870
using the platform.

00:07:19.870 --> 00:07:22.070
Also today, as I talk
through the roadmap,

00:07:22.070 --> 00:07:25.300
I want to take you through four
different kinds of use cases

00:07:25.300 --> 00:07:28.580
that organizations
use, or scenarios.

00:07:28.580 --> 00:07:32.200
One is with modernizing your
data analytics with BigQuery,

00:07:32.200 --> 00:07:34.690
data lakes for
Dataproc and Composer,

00:07:34.690 --> 00:07:38.980
more responsive businesses
with Dataflow and Pub/Sub,

00:07:38.980 --> 00:07:42.100
and then finally,
Enterprise BI with Looker.

00:07:42.100 --> 00:07:43.960
Let's talk about BigQuery.

00:07:43.960 --> 00:07:46.360
BigQuery is highly
scalable cloud data

00:07:46.360 --> 00:07:47.710
warehousing solution.

00:07:47.710 --> 00:07:50.800
We have customers that have
few terabytes to hundreds

00:07:50.800 --> 00:07:53.710
of petabytes within BigQuery.

00:07:53.710 --> 00:07:55.120
It's completely serverless.

00:07:55.120 --> 00:07:58.900
You bring data in, load
it, and start query.

00:07:58.900 --> 00:08:01.360
You don't have to worry
about machines and operations

00:08:01.360 --> 00:08:04.280
and manageability or
anything like that.

00:08:04.280 --> 00:08:06.610
It allows you to do
real-time streaming.

00:08:06.610 --> 00:08:09.850
We have customers that are
ingesting tens of millions

00:08:09.850 --> 00:08:11.590
of events every day--

00:08:11.590 --> 00:08:14.050
sorry, tens of millions
of events every second

00:08:14.050 --> 00:08:15.580
into BigQuery.

00:08:15.580 --> 00:08:17.380
We have embedded
machine learning.

00:08:17.380 --> 00:08:20.590
And then we also enable
insights for everyone

00:08:20.590 --> 00:08:21.980
within organization.

00:08:21.980 --> 00:08:24.280
With investments like
Connected Sheets,

00:08:24.280 --> 00:08:27.470
now data and insights is
accessible to everyone.

00:08:27.470 --> 00:08:30.490
One of the other innovations
that we have worked on

00:08:30.490 --> 00:08:31.990
is around the pricing.

00:08:31.990 --> 00:08:35.350
So BigQuery is 10 years
old in June this year.

00:08:35.350 --> 00:08:37.750
We became 10 years.

00:08:37.750 --> 00:08:42.100
We pioneered the pricing
model of pay per query.

00:08:42.100 --> 00:08:44.380
But over a period of
time, organizations

00:08:44.380 --> 00:08:46.190
have asked for
different options.

00:08:46.190 --> 00:08:48.910
So we launched BigQuery
flat rate, which

00:08:48.910 --> 00:08:51.130
is subscription
pricing every month,

00:08:51.130 --> 00:08:53.050
fixed price, so that
you can go ahead

00:08:53.050 --> 00:08:57.160
and leverage BigQuery with
a consistent price point.

00:08:57.160 --> 00:08:59.750
We also had a lot
of customers ask us

00:08:59.750 --> 00:09:02.620
for this burst per second,
per minute billing so

00:09:02.620 --> 00:09:04.720
that they can bring
workloads that are bursty.

00:09:04.720 --> 00:09:08.510
Monday morning, ETL
jobs have to run.

00:09:08.510 --> 00:09:12.040
Or you have Black Friday,
even, that happens in the US.

00:09:12.040 --> 00:09:14.740
So all of these different
kinds of use cases

00:09:14.740 --> 00:09:16.930
needed this burst capability.

00:09:16.930 --> 00:09:20.700
The beauty of three pricing
models with BigQuery

00:09:20.700 --> 00:09:23.230
is you can actually
interchange them.

00:09:23.230 --> 00:09:25.840
You can basically
use all three of them

00:09:25.840 --> 00:09:28.780
in a mix-and-match fashion
within your organization

00:09:28.780 --> 00:09:32.320
and go ahead and
leverage each one of them

00:09:32.320 --> 00:09:34.150
for different
kinds of use cases.

00:09:34.150 --> 00:09:37.400
So you can easily
mix and match these.

00:09:37.400 --> 00:09:39.640
Let's talk about
BigQuery roadmap.

00:09:39.640 --> 00:09:41.800
I want to highlight a
few of the key things.

00:09:41.800 --> 00:09:43.930
But there is another session.

00:09:43.930 --> 00:09:46.750
At the bottom of the screen, you
will see these recommendations

00:09:46.750 --> 00:09:49.210
I have for different sessions
that give you a deep dive

00:09:49.210 --> 00:09:51.100
into the specific areas.

00:09:51.100 --> 00:09:53.920
But let's talk about the BI
Engine and materialized views,

00:09:53.920 --> 00:09:57.520
always fast, always fresh,
BigQuery ML, Data QnA,

00:09:57.520 --> 00:10:00.350
in Omni today.

00:10:00.350 --> 00:10:02.410
Always fast, always
fresh-- this is

00:10:02.410 --> 00:10:05.530
our theme around collecting
large-scale events

00:10:05.530 --> 00:10:06.790
in real time.

00:10:06.790 --> 00:10:08.740
We have rebuilt our
whole storage engine

00:10:08.740 --> 00:10:10.270
to be streaming first.

00:10:10.270 --> 00:10:13.000
Along with that, we
have materialized

00:10:13.000 --> 00:10:16.920
views, where what
users can do is

00:10:16.920 --> 00:10:19.230
they can take this data
that's streaming, coming in

00:10:19.230 --> 00:10:21.280
or any batch of
data that comes in.

00:10:21.280 --> 00:10:22.830
You can partition
and cluster it so

00:10:22.830 --> 00:10:24.510
that the queries become faster.

00:10:24.510 --> 00:10:27.180
But with materialized
views, you can actually

00:10:27.180 --> 00:10:30.480
preaggregate and keep the
data sets ready for the use

00:10:30.480 --> 00:10:32.205
case that needs low latency.

00:10:34.740 --> 00:10:36.800
One of the best things
about materialized views

00:10:36.800 --> 00:10:39.960
is, as the underneath data
changes, we automatically

00:10:39.960 --> 00:10:41.430
update your materialized view.

00:10:41.430 --> 00:10:43.350
You do not have to
worry about, like,

00:10:43.350 --> 00:10:44.820
running scheduled jobs or not.

00:10:44.820 --> 00:10:46.900
We will automatically
materialize it.

00:10:46.900 --> 00:10:51.120
Second thing we do is, if
a query is written after--

00:10:51.120 --> 00:10:54.570
if you have an old query that
doesn't use materialized view,

00:10:54.570 --> 00:10:56.550
but you've created
this materialized view,

00:10:56.550 --> 00:10:59.580
internally, our query engine
will read out the queries

00:10:59.580 --> 00:11:01.750
to go through materialized view.

00:11:01.750 --> 00:11:03.332
So that's a really
good capability

00:11:03.332 --> 00:11:05.040
so you don't have to
worry about changing

00:11:05.040 --> 00:11:06.210
all your queries at all.

00:11:06.210 --> 00:11:09.140
And finally,
BigQuery BI Engine--

00:11:09.140 --> 00:11:12.840
so BI Engine allows you to
have this in-memory caching

00:11:12.840 --> 00:11:14.850
layer on top of
BigQuery that gives you

00:11:14.850 --> 00:11:18.690
subsecond latency response
times on your BI use cases.

00:11:18.690 --> 00:11:20.760
Today, it only works
with Data Studio.

00:11:20.760 --> 00:11:22.800
But later in the
year, we will make

00:11:22.800 --> 00:11:25.950
it available through
Double SQL, which

00:11:25.950 --> 00:11:28.650
is our internal name
for our SQL capability.

00:11:28.650 --> 00:11:31.200
So any query coming
to BigQuery will

00:11:31.200 --> 00:11:33.570
be able to leverage BI Engine.

00:11:33.570 --> 00:11:36.420
So this will be available
through on the BI tools, JDBC

00:11:36.420 --> 00:11:39.060
drivers, as well as the
queries that you fire.

00:11:39.060 --> 00:11:43.300
It will give you really fast
response times on top of that.

00:11:43.300 --> 00:11:46.270
And we will also support
some of the star schemas

00:11:46.270 --> 00:11:48.610
for BI Engine use cases.

00:11:48.610 --> 00:11:50.630
Another thing is nothing
changes on your side.

00:11:50.630 --> 00:11:51.600
Write the same query.

00:11:51.600 --> 00:11:54.390
We will automatically fall
down to whichever layer has

00:11:54.390 --> 00:11:56.530
the most cache results for you.

00:11:56.530 --> 00:11:59.430
So if it's BI Engine in-memory
caching, we'll use that.

00:11:59.430 --> 00:12:01.170
If not, we'll fall
to materialized view.

00:12:01.170 --> 00:12:04.010
If not, to partition
and cluster.

00:12:04.010 --> 00:12:06.810
Let's switch gears,
talk about BigQuery ML.

00:12:06.810 --> 00:12:10.230
BigQuery ML is our embedded
machine learning capability.

00:12:10.230 --> 00:12:12.240
It allows you to write
two lines of code

00:12:12.240 --> 00:12:14.670
to create machine
learning models

00:12:14.670 --> 00:12:17.670
and then get predictions
with similar kind of tool.

00:12:17.670 --> 00:12:19.330
There are two lines of code.

00:12:19.330 --> 00:12:22.745
So we launched this a
year and a half back.

00:12:22.745 --> 00:12:24.120
And then since
then, we have been

00:12:24.120 --> 00:12:27.000
adding all these
different types of models.

00:12:27.000 --> 00:12:30.360
We have seen tremendous
interest in k-means clustering.

00:12:30.360 --> 00:12:32.760
We've made matrix
recommendations,

00:12:32.760 --> 00:12:35.320
or recommendations for
matrix factorization.

00:12:35.320 --> 00:12:39.440
We have customers who have taken
their historical user data,

00:12:39.440 --> 00:12:43.830
gone ahead and create clusters
based on what type of users

00:12:43.830 --> 00:12:44.400
are there.

00:12:44.400 --> 00:12:46.860
And then our two product
recommendations to them

00:12:46.860 --> 00:12:49.500
were using matrix factorization.

00:12:49.500 --> 00:12:52.670
In recent times, we've also
added new capabilities,

00:12:52.670 --> 00:12:56.790
where now you can connect
to Cloud AI services

00:12:56.790 --> 00:12:59.170
and their capabilities,
like AutoML Tables.

00:12:59.170 --> 00:13:01.730
Now, BigQuery ML,
with two lines of SQL,

00:13:01.730 --> 00:13:02.730
you can leverage AutoML.

00:13:02.730 --> 00:13:04.760
And you can use
XGBoost from there.

00:13:04.760 --> 00:13:07.050
And you can use
DNN models with it.

00:13:07.050 --> 00:13:10.860
So we are basically making it--

00:13:10.860 --> 00:13:12.720
all the different
Cloud AI capabilities

00:13:12.720 --> 00:13:16.530
more accessible through SQL
for our BigQuery and our data

00:13:16.530 --> 00:13:18.760
analyst community.

00:13:18.760 --> 00:13:20.490
We also have the
time-series forecasting.

00:13:20.490 --> 00:13:22.890
But the most important
thing that we have added

00:13:22.890 --> 00:13:25.470
is this interoperability
with TensorFlow.

00:13:25.470 --> 00:13:29.160
Now you can take the model
that you created in BigQuery,

00:13:29.160 --> 00:13:31.200
export it into
TensorFlow, and then

00:13:31.200 --> 00:13:33.690
load it into your
serving platform

00:13:33.690 --> 00:13:36.080
and make it more accessible
in the real-time world.

00:13:38.860 --> 00:13:42.620
The other theme we have had is
this democratizing insights.

00:13:42.620 --> 00:13:45.750
With Connected Sheets, you can
take a petabyte-scale table

00:13:45.750 --> 00:13:48.960
and then have a great
visualization, great analysis

00:13:48.960 --> 00:13:53.160
with Connected Sheets
or GSuite Sheets.

00:13:53.160 --> 00:13:56.340
With Data QnA, now you
can use natural language

00:13:56.340 --> 00:13:58.840
to get your questions answered.

00:13:58.840 --> 00:14:03.020
So it's basically, you just ask
the question, get an answer.

00:14:03.020 --> 00:14:06.450
This is going to free up a lot
of time for BI teams, right?

00:14:06.450 --> 00:14:08.980
You don't have to now create
a custom dashboard order

00:14:08.980 --> 00:14:10.770
report for every
question that comes up.

00:14:10.770 --> 00:14:12.670
You can just ask it.

00:14:12.670 --> 00:14:14.580
The main thing that
we're focused on

00:14:14.580 --> 00:14:18.510
is making this available through
various different experiences.

00:14:18.510 --> 00:14:21.660
We do realize organizations
want different experiences

00:14:21.660 --> 00:14:23.100
with natural language.

00:14:23.100 --> 00:14:25.800
In many cases, it's all
available through an API.

00:14:25.800 --> 00:14:29.420
So organizations can build
their own custom experiences.

00:14:29.420 --> 00:14:31.580
We will make it available
through Google Sheets.

00:14:31.580 --> 00:14:34.357
We will have some chat bots,
dialog flow integration.

00:14:34.357 --> 00:14:35.940
So all of these
different integrations

00:14:35.940 --> 00:14:37.950
are planned to be coming.

00:14:37.950 --> 00:14:41.310
It's all built on an internal
technology called Analyza.

00:14:41.310 --> 00:14:44.300
There's a great paper on
it if you're interested.

00:14:44.300 --> 00:14:45.900
But this is same
technology that also

00:14:45.900 --> 00:14:50.310
powers Google Assistant and
various different services

00:14:50.310 --> 00:14:52.380
that we have internally.

00:14:52.380 --> 00:14:55.090
It allows us to go ahead and
get question parsing, intent

00:14:55.090 --> 00:14:58.230
identification, and
entity matching, all

00:14:58.230 --> 00:15:01.180
done automatically using
the same technology.

00:15:01.180 --> 00:15:03.240
And then the SQL and
how we map to BigQuery

00:15:03.240 --> 00:15:06.960
is where we are
innovating right now.

00:15:06.960 --> 00:15:09.545
So great new capability
if you're interested.

00:15:09.545 --> 00:15:11.910
Now, you should reach out to
us so that we can give you

00:15:11.910 --> 00:15:13.650
early access and all.

00:15:13.650 --> 00:15:15.060
The other big area--

00:15:15.060 --> 00:15:18.090
every organization is in
multicloud environment now.

00:15:18.090 --> 00:15:20.940
If not, they will be
in sometime to come.

00:15:20.940 --> 00:15:23.910
Few years back, there is always
the question-- which cloud,

00:15:23.910 --> 00:15:25.200
why cloud, and all of that.

00:15:25.200 --> 00:15:29.232
Now I talk to customers
across the globe.

00:15:29.232 --> 00:15:30.690
Most of the questions
that they ask

00:15:30.690 --> 00:15:33.000
is, what are the key
capabilities in which cloud?

00:15:33.000 --> 00:15:36.000
And how do I leverage
them for their use cases?

00:15:36.000 --> 00:15:39.300
So BigQuery Omni is
our ability to take

00:15:39.300 --> 00:15:42.570
some of our core
capabilities, the BigQuery,

00:15:42.570 --> 00:15:45.310
and extend them into
other cloud environments.

00:15:45.310 --> 00:15:49.620
So while you will get
the same experience,

00:15:49.620 --> 00:15:51.300
you'd still go to
the BigQuery UI.

00:15:51.300 --> 00:15:52.980
You still write the
similar queries.

00:15:52.980 --> 00:15:55.480
You're just now accessing
data on different clouds.

00:15:55.480 --> 00:15:57.900
So that's one main thing.

00:15:57.900 --> 00:16:00.420
And then finally, we're
leveraging Anthos internally

00:16:00.420 --> 00:16:02.787
so that the management of
the machines and all of that

00:16:02.787 --> 00:16:03.620
is not your problem.

00:16:03.620 --> 00:16:05.640
We'll just do that
behind the scenes.

00:16:05.640 --> 00:16:09.370
The way to think
about this is, we

00:16:09.370 --> 00:16:11.700
are announcing the
availability on Azure

00:16:11.700 --> 00:16:14.400
in the early alpha
phases, where, really, you

00:16:14.400 --> 00:16:18.840
can reach out to us, and you can
start testing and validations

00:16:18.840 --> 00:16:19.990
and stuff like that.

00:16:19.990 --> 00:16:23.610
We will make it available to
organizations and form a beta

00:16:23.610 --> 00:16:25.890
and all later in
the year and all

00:16:25.890 --> 00:16:29.723
But the way to think about it
is, it's just another region.

00:16:29.723 --> 00:16:31.890
So you would have the same
control plane, same query

00:16:31.890 --> 00:16:33.390
experience in BigQuery.

00:16:33.390 --> 00:16:35.760
But today, you can
connect to US and EU

00:16:35.760 --> 00:16:38.040
multiregion or one of
the regional deployments

00:16:38.040 --> 00:16:39.750
of BigQuery data sets.

00:16:39.750 --> 00:16:43.200
And now the data in S3 just
becomes another data set

00:16:43.200 --> 00:16:44.920
for you to go ahead and query.

00:16:44.920 --> 00:16:48.660
We will take our Compute
Engine and run it in AWS

00:16:48.660 --> 00:16:51.750
so you don't pay any egress
charges back and forth

00:16:51.750 --> 00:16:52.530
within that.

00:16:52.530 --> 00:16:54.630
The only time egress
will be involved

00:16:54.630 --> 00:16:58.260
is when you move that end result
set that we have aggregates

00:16:58.260 --> 00:16:59.650
or something that you created.

00:16:59.650 --> 00:17:01.395
If you want to move
it back into GCP,

00:17:01.395 --> 00:17:02.520
then egress would be there.

00:17:02.520 --> 00:17:04.895
But otherwise, you don't have
to worry about any of that.

00:17:04.895 --> 00:17:06.119
All that is managed service.

00:17:06.119 --> 00:17:08.290
We take care of all that stuff.

00:17:08.290 --> 00:17:12.099
AWS is available now, and
Azure will be coming soon

00:17:12.099 --> 00:17:14.050
in coming quarters.

00:17:14.050 --> 00:17:17.530
So let's switch gears now
and look at the second topic.

00:17:17.530 --> 00:17:21.224
We covered BigQuery from
an analytics perspective.

00:17:21.224 --> 00:17:26.910
Now let's go to our data
lake side of things.

00:17:26.910 --> 00:17:30.450
If you think about Google Cloud,
some of the largest data lakes

00:17:30.450 --> 00:17:34.110
are running on the top of GCP.

00:17:34.110 --> 00:17:37.110
And customers like
Twitter and Pandora--

00:17:37.110 --> 00:17:40.470
all of them run
large-scale clusters on us.

00:17:40.470 --> 00:17:42.540
And the reason is
it's super fast.

00:17:42.540 --> 00:17:43.420
It's secure.

00:17:43.420 --> 00:17:43.980
It's managed.

00:17:43.980 --> 00:17:46.680
And it's cost effective
for organizations.

00:17:46.680 --> 00:17:48.410
And we do support
managed experience

00:17:48.410 --> 00:17:52.620
around various different Apache
projects like Spark and Hadoop

00:17:52.620 --> 00:17:55.470
and Scala and Presto and Druid.

00:17:55.470 --> 00:17:59.650
And I just pick one, like enable
you to run Ranger, all of that.

00:17:59.650 --> 00:18:02.250
And also, we have
managed Airflow,

00:18:02.250 --> 00:18:06.960
which is available as Composer
service that is there.

00:18:06.960 --> 00:18:11.700
Then I, today, wanted to
talk about a few key roadmap

00:18:11.700 --> 00:18:13.200
items in this area.

00:18:13.200 --> 00:18:14.820
One is enterprise notebooks.

00:18:14.820 --> 00:18:17.460
Second is Dataproc
metastore, and then

00:18:17.460 --> 00:18:19.110
analytics on Kubernetes.

00:18:19.110 --> 00:18:22.620
Again, more details
another session.

00:18:22.620 --> 00:18:25.120
We launched our Cloud AI
notebooks in recent past,

00:18:25.120 --> 00:18:25.620
right?

00:18:25.620 --> 00:18:26.610
It's available.

00:18:26.610 --> 00:18:29.110
Organizations are using
it across the board.

00:18:29.110 --> 00:18:30.972
You can have your
personal notebook.

00:18:30.972 --> 00:18:32.430
You can create a
team notebook with

00:18:32.430 --> 00:18:34.770
Kubernetes-backed environment.

00:18:34.770 --> 00:18:37.110
But what we have launched now--

00:18:37.110 --> 00:18:39.890
it's in beta-- that's available
is our Smart Analytics

00:18:39.890 --> 00:18:40.760
framework.

00:18:40.760 --> 00:18:44.060
And what that is, is we are
bringing on the big data

00:18:44.060 --> 00:18:47.990
capabilities, big data services,
behind-the-scene Cloud AI

00:18:47.990 --> 00:18:50.750
notebooks environment, and
enabling you to run this

00:18:50.750 --> 00:18:52.190
on top of a Dataproc cluster.

00:18:52.190 --> 00:18:57.380
So basically, large-scale Spark
jobs can now run on top of it.

00:18:57.380 --> 00:18:59.270
You just go and
create a notebook

00:18:59.270 --> 00:19:01.520
from Dataproc environment.

00:19:01.520 --> 00:19:03.330
And then it's available.

00:19:03.330 --> 00:19:05.480
And then you also have
the Beam notebook.

00:19:05.480 --> 00:19:09.980
And it also connects with
BigQuery, Dataflow, Dataproc.

00:19:09.980 --> 00:19:12.530
All of these engines
become accessible to you

00:19:12.530 --> 00:19:14.330
from the same thing.

00:19:14.330 --> 00:19:16.070
Really powerful,
really looking forward

00:19:16.070 --> 00:19:19.880
to enabling these data
scientists across the globe

00:19:19.880 --> 00:19:23.210
to leverage this new
environment that's available.

00:19:23.210 --> 00:19:24.710
The next topic I
want to talk about

00:19:24.710 --> 00:19:27.350
is managed Dataproc
metastore, or Hive metastore.

00:19:27.350 --> 00:19:30.090
Lot of organizations
have told us managing

00:19:30.090 --> 00:19:32.760
the Hive metastore is painful.

00:19:32.760 --> 00:19:34.670
We should just have a
managed version of it.

00:19:34.670 --> 00:19:36.110
That's exactly what we're doing.

00:19:36.110 --> 00:19:38.450
It's going to be completely
integrated with our security

00:19:38.450 --> 00:19:45.000
capability, monitoring
capability in Google Cloud.

00:19:45.000 --> 00:19:49.340
It will support all the
major security capabilities

00:19:49.340 --> 00:19:50.900
that organizations need.

00:19:50.900 --> 00:19:53.240
Over time, we will
enable all of that stuff.

00:19:53.240 --> 00:19:54.370
So that is there.

00:19:54.370 --> 00:19:56.807
Then you will be able to
go ahead and scale out

00:19:56.807 --> 00:19:57.890
over time and all of that.

00:19:57.890 --> 00:20:02.360
So it's available
in beta this year.

00:20:02.360 --> 00:20:04.610
And so I would highly
recommend using this.

00:20:04.610 --> 00:20:07.280
If you're running any kind
of a Hadoop environment

00:20:07.280 --> 00:20:10.430
on top of Hadoop or Spark
environment in Google Cloud,

00:20:10.430 --> 00:20:14.250
you should use the
managed metastore now.

00:20:14.250 --> 00:20:17.840
And finally, I'm super excited
about this transition from YARN

00:20:17.840 --> 00:20:21.890
to Kubernetes in
the big data world.

00:20:21.890 --> 00:20:26.170
And we're binding some of
it with our launch of Spark

00:20:26.170 --> 00:20:26.960
on Kubernetes.

00:20:26.960 --> 00:20:32.660
And we believe that this is the
best next step for open source

00:20:32.660 --> 00:20:34.910
community to adopt Kubernetes.

00:20:34.910 --> 00:20:38.490
And so right now, we have
Spark on Kubernetes available.

00:20:38.490 --> 00:20:42.770
We are looking at other
options like Flink and Presto.

00:20:42.770 --> 00:20:47.630
But this is a great way of
running Spark environment where

00:20:47.630 --> 00:20:50.720
Kubernetes becomes the backbone
for management and all.

00:20:50.720 --> 00:20:52.220
One of the other
things this enables

00:20:52.220 --> 00:20:56.560
is for you to now run Spark
jobs on top of BigQuery.

00:20:56.560 --> 00:20:59.720
Earlier, I talked about
how BigQuery enables

00:20:59.720 --> 00:21:02.000
you to have the analytical
storage accessible

00:21:02.000 --> 00:21:05.130
from Dataproc and Beam.

00:21:05.130 --> 00:21:07.670
With this now, you can
have Spark on Kubernetes

00:21:07.670 --> 00:21:09.350
connected to BigQuery.

00:21:09.350 --> 00:21:13.700
You can run large-scale Spark ML
jobs or various different kinds

00:21:13.700 --> 00:21:15.530
of aggregates that
you may want to run

00:21:15.530 --> 00:21:20.210
outside of BigQuery SQL,
so great way of doing that.

00:21:20.210 --> 00:21:23.960
With that, let me actually
switch gears and talk

00:21:23.960 --> 00:21:26.910
about the next
topic that we have,

00:21:26.910 --> 00:21:30.940
which is around building
more responsive businesses

00:21:30.940 --> 00:21:33.600
with real-time
environment, right?

00:21:33.600 --> 00:21:36.650
So we covered BigQuery,
analytical cloud data

00:21:36.650 --> 00:21:37.630
warehousing.

00:21:37.630 --> 00:21:39.330
And we covered data lakes.

00:21:39.330 --> 00:21:43.105
Now let's talk about now
Dataflow and Pub/Sub.

00:21:43.105 --> 00:21:46.310
If you look at real time,
it's becoming really critical.

00:21:46.310 --> 00:21:47.750
There's a standard pattern.

00:21:47.750 --> 00:21:51.200
In just large-scale events,
you want to transform them.

00:21:51.200 --> 00:21:53.850
And you want to analyze
and solve them later.

00:21:53.850 --> 00:21:56.750
And so the pattern
normally in GCP--

00:21:56.750 --> 00:21:58.990
the way it works
is you use Pub/Sub

00:21:58.990 --> 00:22:00.230
for large-scale connection.

00:22:00.230 --> 00:22:01.670
You can always use Kafka.

00:22:01.670 --> 00:22:04.160
Confluent has a good
managed service on that.

00:22:04.160 --> 00:22:06.530
You can transform
and transformations.

00:22:06.530 --> 00:22:09.080
Normally, Dataflow
is the best engine,

00:22:09.080 --> 00:22:13.580
I think, across all clouds
for running large-scale stream

00:22:13.580 --> 00:22:14.780
processing.

00:22:14.780 --> 00:22:17.850
And then you can also use
Spark if you wanted do.

00:22:17.850 --> 00:22:20.000
And then for analyzing
and storing later,

00:22:20.000 --> 00:22:21.530
you can use Cloud ML.

00:22:21.530 --> 00:22:22.635
You can use BigQuery.

00:22:22.635 --> 00:22:23.510
You can use Bigtable.

00:22:23.510 --> 00:22:26.330
There are a lot of
different options there.

00:22:26.330 --> 00:22:29.460
In the roadmap side,
I wanted to highlight

00:22:29.460 --> 00:22:31.260
a few of the key things here.

00:22:31.260 --> 00:22:34.130
One was Pub/Sub Lite,
Dataflow SQL making

00:22:34.130 --> 00:22:38.250
it easy to get started
with streaming pipelines,

00:22:38.250 --> 00:22:40.250
observability and
manageability, and, of course,

00:22:40.250 --> 00:22:41.540
streaming predictions.

00:22:41.540 --> 00:22:46.010
Again, more details in
another session in 101.

00:22:46.010 --> 00:22:49.250
If you think about a
collection of events,

00:22:49.250 --> 00:22:51.590
Pub/Sub is an amazing
global service.

00:22:51.590 --> 00:22:53.150
As I said, Spotify uses it.

00:22:53.150 --> 00:22:55.560
Large-scale organizations
are using it.

00:22:55.560 --> 00:23:04.670
But we've seen adoption of lot
of do-it-yourself messaging

00:23:04.670 --> 00:23:06.170
platforms on GCP.

00:23:06.170 --> 00:23:07.820
A lot of organizations
have said, hey,

00:23:07.820 --> 00:23:09.650
we want a lower-cost option.

00:23:09.650 --> 00:23:12.170
We want something to
get started in smaller

00:23:12.170 --> 00:23:14.210
teams, departmental use cases.

00:23:14.210 --> 00:23:16.170
And then Pub/Sub Lite
is exactly for that.

00:23:16.170 --> 00:23:17.390
It's a zonal service.

00:23:17.390 --> 00:23:20.682
It's super low cost so that
organizations can get started.

00:23:20.682 --> 00:23:22.640
They don't have to manage
their infrastructure.

00:23:22.640 --> 00:23:27.952
It's a fully managed, really
low price and low cost offering.

00:23:27.952 --> 00:23:29.660
I would actually
challenge you to compare

00:23:29.660 --> 00:23:32.660
your do-it-yourself costs
against Pub/Sub Lite.

00:23:32.660 --> 00:23:34.160
And I think Pub/Sub
Lite will become

00:23:34.160 --> 00:23:37.320
much more compelling for
you even in those scenarios.

00:23:37.320 --> 00:23:39.490
It's a zonal service.

00:23:39.490 --> 00:23:41.350
So keep that-- let
you know you should

00:23:41.350 --> 00:23:44.200
have that in your failover
scenarios and stuff like that.

00:23:44.200 --> 00:23:46.450
Think through what use
cases you're enabling.

00:23:46.450 --> 00:23:49.780
And then it will integrate with
Dataflow and other services

00:23:49.780 --> 00:23:53.802
like Cloud Functions and
all natively out of the box.

00:23:53.802 --> 00:23:55.510
The other thing I want
to highlight today

00:23:55.510 --> 00:23:57.160
is Dataflow SQL.

00:23:57.160 --> 00:23:59.560
We want to make
building/streaming pipeline

00:23:59.560 --> 00:24:00.760
super easy.

00:24:00.760 --> 00:24:03.790
Dataflow SQL allows you
to write SQL statements

00:24:03.790 --> 00:24:06.280
to create complex pipelines.

00:24:06.280 --> 00:24:09.670
You use the same BigQuery
experience for writing SQL.

00:24:09.670 --> 00:24:11.750
It's the same BigQuery UI.

00:24:11.750 --> 00:24:14.510
And some of the innovation
that we've done in BigQuery--

00:24:14.510 --> 00:24:16.400
UI with multi tabs and all--

00:24:16.400 --> 00:24:18.560
I'm super excited
about that too.

00:24:18.560 --> 00:24:21.140
But you can just
write a SQL statement

00:24:21.140 --> 00:24:22.390
and create the whole pipeline.

00:24:22.390 --> 00:24:24.880
And we'll take care of all
of the creation, management,

00:24:24.880 --> 00:24:26.680
all of that after that.

00:24:26.680 --> 00:24:28.300
The things that
it's available today

00:24:28.300 --> 00:24:30.790
is with Pub/Sub as
well as BigQuery.

00:24:30.790 --> 00:24:32.220
We're going to add Kafka.

00:24:32.220 --> 00:24:36.700
We're going to add Bigtable and
Cloud SQL in addition to those.

00:24:36.700 --> 00:24:41.530
And then we also were looking
at UDFs and enabling UDFs.

00:24:41.530 --> 00:24:43.930
Creating pipelines--
making it easy is great.

00:24:43.930 --> 00:24:45.670
But a lot of
organizations want us

00:24:45.670 --> 00:24:51.250
to go ahead and make
manageability and operational

00:24:51.250 --> 00:24:53.840
management of these
pipelines really easy.

00:24:53.840 --> 00:24:57.880
So we just launched our
observability dashboards

00:24:57.880 --> 00:24:59.420
early in the year.

00:24:59.420 --> 00:25:01.720
Managed pipelines
is coming too, which

00:25:01.720 --> 00:25:04.420
allows you to take any
pipeline, go dig deeper

00:25:04.420 --> 00:25:06.820
into individual steps
of what happened

00:25:06.820 --> 00:25:08.330
and what the
bottleneck was and how

00:25:08.330 --> 00:25:09.580
do you resolve the bottleneck.

00:25:09.580 --> 00:25:13.650
So a lot of that stuff is
getting better and better

00:25:13.650 --> 00:25:14.780
with time.

00:25:14.780 --> 00:25:18.090
Finally, two of the key
trends in Google Cloud

00:25:18.090 --> 00:25:21.010
is streaming and
AI services, which

00:25:21.010 --> 00:25:22.255
is machine learning and AI.

00:25:22.255 --> 00:25:24.880
And we are bringing
both of them together

00:25:24.880 --> 00:25:29.170
with Dataflow, who are enabling
various different integrations

00:25:29.170 --> 00:25:31.930
with Cloud AI,
APIs, and services,

00:25:31.930 --> 00:25:34.180
integration with
TensorFlow Extended.

00:25:34.180 --> 00:25:37.210
Now you can build streaming
pipelines, which basically

00:25:37.210 --> 00:25:40.060
span from collect
large amount of events,

00:25:40.060 --> 00:25:44.350
go ahead and transform them,
do the prediction in real time,

00:25:44.350 --> 00:25:48.900
and then go ahead and serve
it from a serving layer.

00:25:48.900 --> 00:25:51.250
We're not just stopping
at just base integration.

00:25:51.250 --> 00:25:53.410
We're going to have
temporalized solutions

00:25:53.410 --> 00:25:54.850
like anomaly detection.

00:25:54.850 --> 00:25:57.160
But you can take
it and customize it

00:25:57.160 --> 00:25:58.902
for your industry,
your use case,

00:25:58.902 --> 00:26:00.110
and then go ahead and use it.

00:26:00.110 --> 00:26:03.220
We have seen anomaly detection
being used for fraud detection

00:26:03.220 --> 00:26:06.220
in financial services
or e-commerce sites.

00:26:06.220 --> 00:26:12.100
We have seen that in network,
anomaly detection in telecom,

00:26:12.100 --> 00:26:13.120
and all of that.

00:26:13.120 --> 00:26:15.250
So these standardized
patterns and templates

00:26:15.250 --> 00:26:18.490
are going to help organization
to adopt it faster.

00:26:18.490 --> 00:26:22.030
Finally, we are also going
to enable GPUs and TPUs

00:26:22.030 --> 00:26:24.640
through Dataflow pipelines.

00:26:24.640 --> 00:26:28.300
This has been one of the
most asked-for capabilities.

00:26:28.300 --> 00:26:30.540
So you will have
that capability, too.

00:26:30.540 --> 00:26:32.870
So we covered BigQuery,
the data warehousing.

00:26:32.870 --> 00:26:33.860
We covered data lakes.

00:26:33.860 --> 00:26:35.960
We covered streaming analytics.

00:26:35.960 --> 00:26:39.770
Let's switch gears to enterprise
BI and data applications.

00:26:39.770 --> 00:26:44.300
So Looker now is part of
the Google Cloud family.

00:26:44.300 --> 00:26:49.260
It's a great data
platform that's available.

00:26:49.260 --> 00:26:52.700
And basically, Looker is
based on this amazing semantic

00:26:52.700 --> 00:26:53.870
modeling layer.

00:26:53.870 --> 00:26:56.420
It allows you to define
your common understanding

00:26:56.420 --> 00:26:58.120
of your metrics within
the organization.

00:26:58.120 --> 00:26:59.870
And this is one of the
biggest pain points

00:26:59.870 --> 00:27:01.610
for most of our customers.

00:27:01.610 --> 00:27:04.280
They basically have
different-- like,

00:27:04.280 --> 00:27:06.540
since service BI
enabled everybody

00:27:06.540 --> 00:27:09.350
to define their own
dashboards, but also

00:27:09.350 --> 00:27:13.790
everybody to have their own
definition of their metrics.

00:27:13.790 --> 00:27:17.450
So with Looker, you basically
define your common metrics.

00:27:17.450 --> 00:27:19.790
This allows
organizations to rally

00:27:19.790 --> 00:27:23.570
behind the common understanding
of these key metrics.

00:27:23.570 --> 00:27:26.870
It also has this BI capability
with great visualizations

00:27:26.870 --> 00:27:29.395
and great tooling for your
dashboards and reports and all.

00:27:29.395 --> 00:27:32.035
So that's always there.

00:27:32.035 --> 00:27:33.410
Another thing it
allows you to do

00:27:33.410 --> 00:27:35.480
is everything in
Looker is an API.

00:27:35.480 --> 00:27:39.020
So you can build your own custom
applications and custom use

00:27:39.020 --> 00:27:41.418
cases and workflows
on top of Looker.

00:27:41.418 --> 00:27:42.960
Some of the organization
using Looker

00:27:42.960 --> 00:27:46.420
are, like, you can't even
see anything in Looker there.

00:27:46.420 --> 00:27:50.000
It's just an API,
headless BI platform

00:27:50.000 --> 00:27:52.460
that you can go
ahead and do whatever

00:27:52.460 --> 00:27:54.080
UIs you want on top of it.

00:27:54.080 --> 00:27:56.150
And then we've seen a
lot of organizations

00:27:56.150 --> 00:27:58.770
use embedded
Looker, where Looker

00:27:58.770 --> 00:28:01.490
gets embedded into other
applications and all.

00:28:01.490 --> 00:28:04.880
It works with more than 55
different kind of SQL dialects,

00:28:04.880 --> 00:28:08.580
means different databases and
data warehouses in the world,

00:28:08.580 --> 00:28:13.200
including BigQuery and some
of the other cloud providers.

00:28:13.200 --> 00:28:15.950
Today, there's actually a
long list of capabilities

00:28:15.950 --> 00:28:17.690
we are adding with Looker.

00:28:17.690 --> 00:28:20.030
There is a great
session that will go

00:28:20.030 --> 00:28:21.360
and deep dive into that.

00:28:21.360 --> 00:28:23.810
But today, I want to highlight
the three key capabilities

00:28:23.810 --> 00:28:25.360
that we're enabling--

00:28:25.360 --> 00:28:29.540
Looker on GCP, integration
with the BI Engine,

00:28:29.540 --> 00:28:32.360
and then instant exploration.

00:28:32.360 --> 00:28:35.510
So first of all, making
Looker available in the Cloud

00:28:35.510 --> 00:28:39.380
console in GCP is one of our
big priorities this year.

00:28:39.380 --> 00:28:42.320
What that will enable you to
do is you can, with one click,

00:28:42.320 --> 00:28:45.500
get access to Looker,
get started, and leverage

00:28:45.500 --> 00:28:46.100
the product.

00:28:46.100 --> 00:28:47.600
But one of the most
important things

00:28:47.600 --> 00:28:51.700
is we are also focusing on all
the key security horizontals

00:28:51.700 --> 00:28:53.130
that you all care about.

00:28:53.130 --> 00:28:57.298
Like, you want CMEK,
and you want VPCSC.

00:28:57.298 --> 00:28:59.090
All of those different
kind of horizontals,

00:28:59.090 --> 00:29:00.132
they will come over time.

00:29:00.132 --> 00:29:03.160
But that's part of
being a Google service,

00:29:03.160 --> 00:29:04.880
where we're focused on that.

00:29:04.880 --> 00:29:06.830
The second thing is
better integration

00:29:06.830 --> 00:29:11.050
with BigQuery is, of course,
one of our priorities.

00:29:11.050 --> 00:29:14.900
Now BigQuery plus Looker
is an amazing combination.

00:29:14.900 --> 00:29:17.660
Like, there is unlimited
scale on one side

00:29:17.660 --> 00:29:20.930
and then great visual
capabilities but common APIs

00:29:20.930 --> 00:29:22.730
that you can use
semantic modeling layer.

00:29:22.730 --> 00:29:24.640
It's really good story together.

00:29:24.640 --> 00:29:27.950
And with BI Engine, our
in-memory caching layer,

00:29:27.950 --> 00:29:30.770
you can now start bringing
these two together

00:29:30.770 --> 00:29:34.310
and get subsecond latencies
on most of your dashboarding

00:29:34.310 --> 00:29:36.710
and reporting use cases or
different visualizations

00:29:36.710 --> 00:29:39.080
you want to go over.

00:29:39.080 --> 00:29:43.430
Finally, in Looker, you do
have to start with a model,

00:29:43.430 --> 00:29:45.110
define it in LookML.

00:29:45.110 --> 00:29:48.080
And we wanted to make it
very easy for organizations

00:29:48.080 --> 00:29:52.340
and analysts in organizations
to go from BigQuery

00:29:52.340 --> 00:29:54.380
and start using the
power of Looker.

00:29:54.380 --> 00:29:56.750
And basically what
you will be able to do

00:29:56.750 --> 00:30:00.710
with this instant
exploration capability

00:30:00.710 --> 00:30:03.460
is you can write a
query in BigQuery UI.

00:30:03.460 --> 00:30:07.910
And with one click, you can get
into visualization with Looker.

00:30:07.910 --> 00:30:10.730
You will be able to go ahead
and add calculated fields.

00:30:10.730 --> 00:30:13.160
You can share those
visualizations with your peers

00:30:13.160 --> 00:30:14.482
within the organization.

00:30:14.482 --> 00:30:16.190
And then finally, when
you're ready to go

00:30:16.190 --> 00:30:20.600
through this higher-level
capability with enterprise

00:30:20.600 --> 00:30:24.470
capability with the governed
BI and have this modeling

00:30:24.470 --> 00:30:26.188
layer that you
want to customize,

00:30:26.188 --> 00:30:27.980
then you can go ahead
and upgrade for that.

00:30:27.980 --> 00:30:29.870
But just starting
off and getting

00:30:29.870 --> 00:30:31.930
started with Looker
with BigQuery

00:30:31.930 --> 00:30:33.870
is going to become super simple.

00:30:33.870 --> 00:30:36.930
With a few clicks, you will
be able to go through that.

00:30:36.930 --> 00:30:41.180
We covered a lot of stuff today
across Cloud data warehousing,

00:30:41.180 --> 00:30:45.470
data lakes, streaming analytics,
as well as enterprise BI

00:30:45.470 --> 00:30:46.850
and data applications.

00:30:46.850 --> 00:30:48.590
We have tons of
more stuff coming.

00:30:48.590 --> 00:30:54.020
There are a lot of details that
we have across the portfolio.

00:30:54.020 --> 00:30:59.780
In last few years, we've been
leaders in every report that's

00:30:59.780 --> 00:31:01.330
